  <data key="d2">fact_entity</data>
  <data key="d4">CONCEPT</data>
</edge>
<edge source="2d6cd2a6-4a3a-44fe-aa4e-912059d4a996" target="6a9afb13-0376-4ed6-a2cc-d6e5d5c6b68b">
  <data key="d2">fact_entity</data>
  <data key="d4">DOCUMENT</data>
</edge>
<edge source="2d6cd2a6-4a3a-44fe-aa4e-912059d4a996" target="fb72e3f0-f7b4-42f1-8874-ebac56171d8b">
  <data key="d3">The lack of an automatic premium loan feature means policyholders cannot use it to avoid lapses, even if reinstatement remains an option later.</data>
  <data key="d4">INDEPENDENT</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.0</data>
</edge>
<edge source="2d6cd2a6-4a3a-44fe-aa4e-912059d4a996" target="3aea6d6e-6546-4579-b452-cbd38accf2bf">
  <data key="d3">The lack of an automatic premium loan feature increases the importance of alternative solutions like proving insurability for reinstatement, since borrowers cannot rely on loans to cover missed payments.</data>
  <data key="d4">INDEPENDENT</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.0</data>
</edge>
<edge source="2d6cd2a6-4a3a-44fe-aa4e-912059d4a996" target="e6dc59e3-a8f4-4f56-940d-46199cdc1c83">
  <data key="d3">Since the policy does not have an automatic premium loan, reinstatement depends entirely on paying overdue amounts directly, making timely payment essential.</data>
  <data key="d4">ENHANCES</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.75</data>
</edge>
<edge source="2d6cd2a6-4a3a-44fe-aa4e-912059d4a996" target="afe75990-53e6-4092-8595-3ddaed9d18bc">
  <data key="d3">Without an automatic premium loan feature, policyholders must rely on out-of-pocket payment of overdue premiums plus interest, which explains why interest is assessed at reinstatement.</data>
  <data key="d4">CAUSES</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.85</data>
</edge>
<edge source="2d6cd2a6-4a3a-44fe-aa4e-912059d4a996" target="65a43e84-d268-46ce-900c-06f4538f2516">
  <data key="d3">The lack of an automatic premium loan feature does not affect the display of premium details in the Schedule of Benefits. These elements pertain to separate components of the policy’s design.</data>
  <data key="d4">INDEPENDENT</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.0</data>
</edge>
<edge source="2d6cd2a6-4a3a-44fe-aa4e-912059d4a996" target="d3aeb4da-98f5-49aa-9680-88328bc6317d">
  <data key="d3">The absence of an automatic premium loan means that failure to pay premiums in advance could lead directly to lapses without a built-in buffer, making the "payable in advance" rule more critical for maintaining coverage.</data>
  <data key="d4">DEPENDS ON</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.45</data>
</edge>
<edge source="2d6cd2a6-4a3a-44fe-aa4e-912059d4a996" target="a81e586d-f4c9-49fe-827d-4bb85cdc14e2">
  <data key="d3">The lack of an automatic premium loan feature does not influence the option to pay premiums monthly; it pertains to alternative funding mechanisms rather than payment schedules.</data>
  <data key="d4">INDEPENDENT</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.0</data>
</edge>
<edge source="2d6cd2a6-4a3a-44fe-aa4e-912059d4a996" target="06c6864a-8a1b-46ee-bb2f-99522c008d8a">
  <data key="d3">The absence of an automatic premium loan provision has no bearing on the availability of quarterly premium payments, which remain a distinct choice under the policy’s payment options.</data>
  <data key="d4">INDEPENDENT</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.0</data>
</edge>
<edge source="2d6cd2a6-4a3a-44fe-aa4e-912059d4a996" target="ae290ac1-57d5-418a-aeff-3020cd16c2e5">
  <data key="d3">The absence of an automatic premium loan provision means the policyholder cannot use borrowed funds from the policy’s cash value to cover unpaid premiums. This standalone feature has no bearing on whether premiums can be paid on a semi-annual basis, which remains a distinct choice governed by the payment plan selection.</data>
  <data key="d4">INDEPENDENT</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.0</data>
</edge>
<edge source="2d6cd2a6-4a3a-44fe-aa4e-912059d4a996" target="fc47d11a-7ce2-4140-9e5d-84adfba6efd3">
  <data key="d3">The absence of an automatic premium loan feature indicates a limitation in built-in financial support mechanisms. In contrast, paying premiums annually reflects one of several scheduled payment options permitted under the policy.</data>
  <data key="d4">INDEPENDENT</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.0</data>
</edge>
<edge source="2d6cd2a6-4a3a-44fe-aa4e-912059d4a996" target="67535076-c012-4f51-9fca-3da547334acf">
  <data key="d3">The absence of an automatic premium loan provision does not affect the policyholder’s ability to choose a monthly, quarterly, semiannual, or annual payment mode; these are separate design features of the policy.</data>
  <data key="d4">INDEPENDENT</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.0</data>
</edge>
<edge source="2d6cd2a6-4a3a-44fe-aa4e-912059d4a996" target="2fe7c477-3ff3-4964-8334-5bce147c512e">
  <data key="d3">The absence of an automatic premium loan feature means the policyholder cannot use borrowed funds to cover unpaid premiums, regardless of the approved payment mode. This condition stands independent of whether monthly, quarterly, or annual modes were selected.</data>
  <data key="d4">INDEPENDENT</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.0</data>
</edge>
<edge source="2d6cd2a6-4a3a-44fe-aa4e-912059d4a996" target="f501fcfb-9924-4880-8895-de800216cc6b">
  <data key="d3">The lack of an automatic premium loan feature indicates a restriction on borrowing against the policy’s cash value, but it has no bearing on when the first premium is due. These elements operate independently within the policy framework.</data>
  <data key="d4">NO RELATIONSHIP</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.0</data>
</edge>
<edge source="2d6cd2a6-4a3a-44fe-aa4e-912059d4a996" target="d0a3204c-79af-4db3-91c3-0206bb2cd580">
  <data key="d3">While premiums are required on specific due dates, the lack of an automatic premium loan provision means that failure to pay may lead directly to lapse without alternative protection, increasing the risk associated with missed payments.</data>
  <data key="d4">INDEPENDENT</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.0</data>
</edge>
<edge source="2d6cd2a6-4a3a-44fe-aa4e-912059d4a996" target="a88904d4-aa6d-4deb-9bf5-50550bbc075d">
  <data key="d3">The absence of an automatic premium loan means that alternative mechanisms like adjusting payment schedules cannot compensate for missed payments through loans. However, changing payment frequency does not affect whether a loan option exists.</data>
  <data key="d4">INDEPENDENT</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.0</data>
</edge>
<edge source="2d6cd2a6-4a3a-44fe-aa4e-912059d4a996" target="c9080843-8376-4174-a9be-27f5cfe85dc2">
  <data key="d3">The absence of an automatic premium loan means that alternative mechanisms like adjusting payment frequency cannot compensate for missing payments without additional provisions. However, modifying payment schedules does not imply inclusion of loan features.</data>
  <data key="d4">INDEPENDENT</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.0</data>
</edge>
<edge source="2d6cd2a6-4a3a-44fe-aa4e-912059d4a996" target="7dd274eb-79fa-4549-9a30-e662efe45594">
  <data key="d3">The lack of an automatic premium loan provision does not affect the requirement to submit written notice when changing the premium payment mode.</data>
  <data key="d4">INDEPENDENT</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.0</data>
</edge>
<edge source="2d6cd2a6-4a3a-44fe-aa4e-912059d4a996" target="c8766159-f2f1-4980-a4f4-30a5ac151e22">
  <data key="d3">The lack of an automatic premium loan provision does not affect the process or effectiveness of switching premium modes, which depends solely on insurer approval.</data>
  <data key="d4">INDEPENDENT</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.0</data>
</edge>
<edge source="2d6cd2a6-4a3a-44fe-aa4e-912059d4a996" target="18dba2f2-8e4f-4bcc-9c25-79f3830d6409">
  <data key="d3">The lack of an automatic premium loan means the policy cannot use borrowed funds to cover unpaid premiums. As a result, failure to pay within the grace period leads directly to policy lapse.</data>
  <data key="d4">ENHANCES RISK</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.3</data>
</edge>
<edge source="2d6cd2a6-4a3a-44fe-aa4e-912059d4a996" target="b160aaee-2bb3-42d4-99f1-e1274612ebc3">
  <data key="d3">Without an automatic premium loan, policyholders cannot defer payment using accumulated cash value, raising the chance of lapse and subsequent loss of coverage.</data>
  <data key="d4">CAUSES</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.85</data>
</edge>
<edge source="2d6cd2a6-4a3a-44fe-aa4e-912059d4a996" target="2355dc3f-af2e-4eda-b96d-fd3204fa6b8e">
  <data key="d3">Without an automatic premium loan, failure to pay premiums may result in policy lapse, making the reinstatement provision essential for recovery, thus reinforcing the condition stated in fact_sentence_1.</data>
  <data key="d4">ENHANCES</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.75</data>
</edge>
<edge source="2d6cd2a6-4a3a-44fe-aa4e-912059d4a996" target="cf255e83-6bf3-43a7-a0f5-ffa85d8f1f6d">
  <data key="d3">The absence of an Automatic Premium Loan provision in the base policy means it requires additional documentation to be included. This provision can only be activated through formal addition via a rider or endorsement.</data>
  <data key="d4">EXPLAINS</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.95</data>
</edge>
<edge source="2d6cd2a6-4a3a-44fe-aa4e-912059d4a996" target="3ac57c69-3ec6-47e1-9a1c-8e288edc1b2b">
  <data key="d3">The absence of an Automatic Premium Loan provision means that unpaid premiums after the grace period cannot be covered automatically through a policy loan, rendering the mechanism described in fact_sentence_2 inactive.</data>
  <data key="d4">PREVENTS</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.85</data>
</edge>
<edge source="2d6cd2a6-4a3a-44fe-aa4e-912059d4a996" target="0e6c34e9-34ee-4684-a4db-b8f932cd319e">
  <data key="d3">The absence of an automatic premium loan feature means that any loan used to cover unpaid premiums relies solely on pre-existing loan value rather than being automatically applied.</data>
  <data key="d4">DEPENDS_ON</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.92</data>
</edge>
<edge source="2d6cd2a6-4a3a-44fe-aa4e-912059d4a996" target="defb9349-5ec2-419c-99cf-a721b93806df">
  <data key="d3">The absence of an automatic premium loan feature pertains to how premiums may be managed but does not affect the conditions under which the death benefit is paid after proof of death.</data>
  <data key="d4">INDEPENDENT</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.0</data>
</edge>
<edge source="2d6cd2a6-4a3a-44fe-aa4e-912059d4a996" target="401085bd-ec9b-4194-a332-d312acf2f556">
  <data key="d3">The absence of an automatic premium loan feature relates to how premiums may be handled during the policyholder’s lifetime, whereas the death benefit being paid to the beneficiary pertains to post-death settlement. The two facts do not influence each other logically.</data>
  <data key="d4">INDEPENDENT</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.0</data>
</edge>
<edge source="2d6cd2a6-4a3a-44fe-aa4e-912059d4a996" target="2030c442-4fb0-495a-995b-bc3c20a392ff">
  <data key="d3">The absence of an automatic premium loan feature relates to policyholder financing during active coverage, whereas the lump-sum death benefit pertains to post-death disbursement terms. The two facts concern distinct phases and functions within the policy lifecycle.</data>
  <data key="d4">INDEPENDENT</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.0</data>
</edge>
<edge source="2d6cd2a6-4a3a-44fe-aa4e-912059d4a996" target="e7ae6851-fbec-41a1-96fb-55af6ecf7ebc">
  <data key="d3">The absence of an automatic premium loan feature relates to how premiums are handled during lapses, while the need for company approval of alternative settlement options concerns post-death benefit distribution. The two do not influence each other directly.</data>
  <data key="d4">INDEPENDENT</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.0</data>
</edge>
<edge source="2d6cd2a6-4a3a-44fe-aa4e-912059d4a996" target="b9f4573a-344d-4bda-a387-f3c32ffcf8c5">
  <data key="d3">The lack of an automatic premium loan feature reflects a limitation in policy flexibility, contributing to the overall framework under which the death benefit is governed. This condition reinforces the importance of adherence to policy terms when claiming benefits.</data>
  <data key="d4">CONDITIONS_FOR</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.75</data>
</edge>
<edge source="2d6cd2a6-4a3a-44fe-aa4e-912059d4a996" target="c8957594-3af6-486f-91f2-abe9eabc42e5">
  <data key="d3">The absence of an automatic premium loan feature relates to how premiums are handled when payments are delayed, whereas providing proof of death pertains to validating claims after the insured's passing. The two facts concern separate stages of policy lifecycle management.</data>
  <data key="d4">INDEPENDENT</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.0</data>
</edge>
<edge source="2d6cd2a6-4a3a-44fe-aa4e-912059d4a996" target="38feed0f-4953-4e2f-a66c-0c3a2fbe012d">
  <data key="d3">The absence of an automatic premium loan feature relates to policy benefits and payment flexibility, whereas additional documentation pertains to verification processes. The two do not logically influence each other.</data>
  <data key="d4">NONE</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.0</data>
</edge>
<edge source="2d6cd2a6-4a3a-44fe-aa4e-912059d4a996" target="e15e7155-24f9-4078-bb5f-26d32c0baa61">
  <data key="d3">Fact sentence 1 states that the policy lacks an automatic premium loan feature unless modified by a rider. Fact sentence 2 describes the necessary documentation for processing a death claim. These points relate to different administrative conditions but do not influence each other logically.</data>
  <data key="d4">NONE</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.0</data>
</edge>
