  <data key="d3">The policy owner's right to request beneficiary designations is independent of whether the company assumes liability for assignments.</data>
  <data key="d4"></data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.0</data>
</edge>
<edge source="9a4ee5a5-54a6-4cfd-82d7-4f61d67a4610" target="89ec8af1-a187-47d1-be96-dd8563e25f71">
  <data key="d3">The second sentence establishes the policy owner’s right to set beneficiaries, whereas the first warns about possible tax outcomes from transferring ownership via assignment. The two reflect separate facets of policy management without mutual dependence.</data>
  <data key="d4">NO_RELATIONSHIP</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.0</data>
</edge>
<edge source="9a4ee5a5-54a6-4cfd-82d7-4f61d67a4610" target="f292fa61-3d93-4115-9af6-3725ae88913a">
  <data key="d3">The existence of the owner’s right to request beneficiary designations provides context for why assignments might impose restrictions, as they can override or limit such control over beneficiary choices.</data>
  <data key="d4">PRECEDES</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.45</data>
</edge>
<edge source="9a4ee5a5-54a6-4cfd-82d7-4f61d67a4610" target="f36a1664-7f6f-4d19-a228-86f3f28f271d">
  <data key="d3">The right to designate beneficiaries allows the owner to determine who receives benefits after death. This authority complements the option to cancel the policy, reinforcing overall ownership control over the policy’s lifecycle.</data>
  <data key="d4">COMPLEMENTARY</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.75</data>
</edge>
