<edge source="925dc2ea-b455-4473-b4ed-51ff493dd9eb" target="4ec4c4a4-c570-4478-be1c-c95a2d28b386">
  <data key="d3">Approval by the company is required before a new settlement option takes effect, whereas changing a beneficiary does not require such consent. The two actions differ in their regulatory oversight despite being within the same category of policy adjustments.</data>
  <data key="d4">INDEPENDENT</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.15</data>
</edge>
<edge source="925dc2ea-b455-4473-b4ed-51ff493dd9eb" target="40cbb338-dde2-44ee-9830-c0c26916330c">
  <data key="d3">Fact sentence 2 indicates that altering the settlement method needs company approval, reflecting a broader pattern where significant ownership decisions—including assigning the policy—are subject to corporate validation.</data>
  <data key="d4">SUBJECT_TO_APPROVAL</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.75</data>
</edge>
<edge source="925dc2ea-b455-4473-b4ed-51ff493dd9eb" target="376c30b4-9340-49c7-86af-f148910aff82">
  <data key="d3">The requirement for company approval of settlement changes does not rely on whether assignments are valid, nor does it affect the legal standing of an assignment. The two clauses operate independently under distinct procedural frameworks.</data>
  <data key="d4">INDEPENDENT</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.0</data>
</edge>
<edge source="925dc2ea-b455-4473-b4ed-51ff493dd9eb" target="89ec8af1-a187-47d1-be96-dd8563e25f71">
  <data key="d3">Approval from the company for settling claims does not affect the tax liabilities associated with assigning the policy.</data>
  <data key="d4"></data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.0</data>
</edge>
