</edge>
<edge source="911c8355-e674-438d-8970-674b61225d1c" target="03cfef7f-f2a0-44bd-864f-0344e97bc6d0">
  <data key="d3">Rules about changing settlement options rely on predefined terms, whereas identifying the beneficiary depends solely on formal designations; neither directly influences the other’s validity or execution.</data>
  <data key="d4">INDEPENDENT</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.0</data>
</edge>
<edge source="911c8355-e674-438d-8970-674b61225d1c" target="e6149469-d151-4d22-aff3-fb7f2901e4bd">
  <data key="d3">This statement governs procedural constraints on changing how benefits are settled, independent of who receives the proceeds if no beneficiary survives.</data>
  <data key="d4">INDEPENDENT</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.0</data>
</edge>
<edge source="911c8355-e674-438d-8970-674b61225d1c" target="0c2f9120-31fb-43ab-81cd-fae794c65930">
  <data key="d3">The second sentence establishes that changing the settlement method is conditional on the availability of approved settlement options. This context helps clarify how benefits are distributed but does not affect the outcome described in the first sentence regarding estate disbursement.</data>
  <data key="d4">INDEPENDENT</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.0</data>
</edge>
