  <data key="d3">The statement about interest reducing the death benefit describes a financial mechanism within the rider but does not address illegal behavior or its implications.</data>
  <data key="d4"></data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.0</data>
</edge>
<edge source="8690a7f5-82be-413d-a92b-8be70122f5aa" target="f2b2eda1-ace9-416b-a284-68313b16efe9">
  <data key="d3">The first sentence explains how accrued interest affects the remaining death benefit after an accelerated payment. The second sentence outlines that intentionally hiding key information qualifies as insurance fraud, but it does not directly interact with the financial mechanism described earlier.</data>
  <data key="d4">NO RELATIONSHIP</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.0</data>
</edge>
<edge source="8690a7f5-82be-413d-a92b-8be70122f5aa" target="cfcb3d69-6bc4-465d-b7ca-f58645d5d6ee">
  <data key="d3">The acceleration of benefits includes interest deductions from the death benefit, reflecting financial terms of the rider. This contrasts with the separate concept of committing fraud through misrepresentation of material facts during claims processing.</data>
  <data key="d4">NO_RELATIONSHIP</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.0</data>
</edge>
<edge source="8690a7f5-82be-413d-a92b-8be70122f5aa" target="cd70f299-b66e-49aa-b3c6-69fcc2129242">
  <data key="d3">While not directly altering the policy itself, the application of interest to the accelerated benefit necessitates adherence to written procedures for validation, aligning with the general rule that all changes must be formally documented.</data>
  <data key="d4">CORROBORATES</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.65</data>
</edge>
